---
// The landing page of shapelogic.xyz, kept as the original static page
// (shapelogic.xyz repository); the user guide under /docs/ is Starlight.
import '../styles/landing.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>ShapeLogic</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="ShapeLogic is an open source puzzle solver, a from-scratch successor to BurrTools written with AI."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/puzzle_icon.jpg" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="banner">
      <img src="/assets/banner.png" alt="ShapeLogic" width="2000" height="666" />
    </header>

    <main>
      <h1>A fast-moving, open source puzzle solver.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        ShapeLogic is a small open source tool for designing and solving interlocking and packing
        puzzles. It started as a fork of
        <a href="https://burrtools.sourceforge.net/">BurrTools</a>, the puzzle software created by
        Andreas Röver, and has since been rewritten from the ground up.
      </p>

      <div class="links">
        <a href="/docs/">User Guide <svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 7v14"/><path d="M3 18a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 4 4 0 0 1 4-4h5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-6a3 3 0 0 0-3 3 3 3 0 0 0-3-3z"/></svg></a>
        <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/latest">Download <svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 15V3"/><path d="M21 15v4a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2v-4"/><path d="m7 10 5 5 5-5"/></svg></a>
        <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic">GitHub <svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 13v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/><path d="m15 3 6 0 0 6"/><path d="M10 14 21 3"/></svg></a>
        <a href="/demo/">Demo <svg class="icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4.037 4.688a.495.495 0 0 1 .651-.651l16 6.5a.5.5 0 0 1-.063.947l-6.124 1.58a2 2 0 0 0-1.438 1.435l-1.579 6.126a.5.5 0 0 1-.947.063z"/></svg></a>
      </div>

      <section class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h2>Built by AI</h2>
          <p>The new version was written almost entirely by Claude AI (Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h2>A new frontend</h2>
          <p>
            The interface is built on a web framework inspired by the amazing work of
            <a href="https://github.com/grssam">Girish Sharma</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h2>Rapidly evolving</h2>
          <p>
            The goal is a BurrTools-like tool that is developed quickly and gains new features and
            modes often.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="blog" aria-labelledby="news-heading">
        <h2 class="blog-heading" id="news-heading">News</h2>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="post-v1-5">
          <p class="post-meta">
            <time datetime="2026-10-29">29 October 2026</time> · Release
          </p>
          <h3 id="post-v1-5">ShapeLogic v1.5: assemblies kept, an Export menu, and a new top bar</h3>
          <p>
            Version 1.5 can keep the assemblies that don't come apart, puts exporting on the Solver
            tab, gives sliding puzzles a Boards list and a side-by-side Start and Goal, and brings a
            new top bar.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Keep the assemblies that don't come apart.</strong> The
              <strong>Always show Assemblies</strong> search option lists them after the
              disassemblies, and the solutions list says what each group holds.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>An Export menu on the Solver tab.</strong> The Solutions card's
              <strong>Export</strong> menu exports the shown solution's pieces as STL, and everything
              in <strong>File &#9656; Export</strong>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Clear a problem's result.</strong> <strong>Change&#8230;</strong> on the Puzzle
              tab ends with <strong>Clear Result</strong>, which can be undone.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Sliding puzzles.</strong> Boards have their own <strong>Boards</strong> list on
              the Shapes tab, and <strong>Focus 2D editor</strong> on the Puzzle tab shows Start and
              Goal side by side.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A new top bar.</strong> It grows with the font size, and the new
              <strong>Top bar theme</strong> setting in <strong>Settings &#9656; General</strong>
              chooses <strong>Colorful</strong>, with the axis colors behind the name and on the tabs,
              or <strong>Boring</strong>.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Free Spin</strong> turns the view smoothly, the text is a size larger by default,
            and the side cards grow with a wide window. Read more in
            <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting started</a>. Many small bug fixes and
            improvements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/tag/v1.5">Release notes and downloads on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="post-v1-4">
          <p class="post-meta">
            <time datetime="2026-10-28">28 October 2026</time> · Release
          </p>
          <h3 id="post-v1-4">ShapeLogic v1.4: one sliding puzzle solver, and new look settings</h3>
          <p>
            Version 1.4 gives sliding puzzles a single solver with no limit on the positions it
            looks at, lets each copy of a piece have its own start and goal, and adds settings for
            how the app looks.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>One sliding puzzle solver.</strong> The <strong>Sliding Puzzle Solver</strong>
              always finds the fewest moves or proves there is no solution, and uses every core. The
              Fast and Deep solvers are gone, and puzzles saved with them open as before.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Several copies of a sliding piece.</strong> Each copy can have its own start
              and goal. A copy without a start can start anywhere, and one without a goal can end
              anywhere.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Boards and pieces side by side.</strong> On the Puzzle tab the START and GOAL
              boards sit at the top of the 3D view with the pieces under them, and starts and goals
              are placed on the new <strong>Start and goal</strong> card. Pieces are larger and
              closer together, and zooming makes every piece larger or smaller.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Board colors that follow the theme.</strong> Sliding boards are dark gray in
              the Light theme and light gray in the Dark theme. <strong>Settings &#9656; Grid
              editor</strong> sets the color for each theme.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>New look settings.</strong> <strong>Font size</strong>, the location of the
              main tab bar, <strong>Compact</strong> density and sidebar widths are in
              <strong>Settings &#9656; General</strong>, and <strong>View &#9656; Color
              Theme</strong> switches between Light, Dark and System.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Memory and disk for big searches.</strong>
              <strong>Settings &#9656; Solver</strong> sets how much memory and disk space a
              sliding or stacking search may use.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The voxel editor can show the layer below or above faintly, <strong>+ New Piece</strong>
            and <strong>+ New Board</strong> take the size of the last one, and About ShapeLogic
            shows what it is built with. Many small bug fixes and improvements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/tag/v1.4">Release notes and downloads on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="post-v1-3">
          <p class="post-meta">
            <time datetime="2026-10-26">26 October 2026</time> · Release
          </p>
          <h3 id="post-v1-3">ShapeLogic v1.3: tidier sliding puzzles and new settings</h3>
          <p>
            Version 1.3 keeps the boards of a sliding puzzle in order, shows piece names in the
            Solver and adds settings for the grid editor and the solver.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Tidier sliding puzzles.</strong> Boards (start and goal positions) now always
              stay at the top of the shapes list, labelled B1, B2 and so on, with the pieces below
              them as S1, S2 and so on. You can name each board, and the name shows in the Puzzle
              tab&rsquo;s problems list.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>New Settings pages.</strong> A <strong>Grid editor</strong> page holds the
              grid plane and the size of a new shape. <strong>Performance</strong> is now called
              <strong>Solver</strong> and adds a <strong>Default solver type</strong>, Complete or
              Fast.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Piece names in the Solver.</strong> The Piece display chips show each
              piece&rsquo;s name, and you can draw each piece&rsquo;s label on it in the 3D view.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Easier voxel editing.</strong> <strong>+ New</strong> makes a shape the size of
              the selected one, you can type a size straight into the X, Y and Z boxes, and hovering
              over a voxel always highlights it in the 3D view.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Open and Save now start in the folder you used last, and Enable High Memory is on by
            default for sliding and stacking puzzles. Many small bug fixes and improvements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Updated to v1.3.1.</strong> ShapeLogic now tells you when a new version is out,
            and <strong>Check for Updates&hellip;</strong> checks at any time. Time used counts every
            run of a solve, including after Continue, and times are shown in words. Sliding puzzles
            get a new shape size of their own, and About shows the version you installed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/docs/shapes-tab/#grid-editor-settings">The Grid editor settings</a>
            ·
            <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/tag/v1.3.1">Release notes and downloads on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="post-v1-2">
          <p class="post-meta">
            <time datetime="2026-10-23">23 October 2026</time> · Release
          </p>
          <h3 id="post-v1-2">ShapeLogic v1.2: vertical moves are an option, and easier 3D editing</h3>
          <p>
            Version 1.2 makes sliding puzzles with several layers faster to solve, and makes
            editing shapes in the 3D view easier.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Vertical moves are now an option.</strong> On a tray with more than one
              layer, pieces now stay in their own layer unless you turn on
              <strong>Allow Vertical Moves</strong> in the Solver tab. Most multi-layer puzzles now
              solve much faster. Turn the option on for puzzles where a piece must be lifted over
              the others.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Easier 3D editing.</strong> In 3D edit mode, clicking different parts of a
              voxel&rsquo;s face adds, changes or deletes it, and you can start drawing an empty
              shape right in the 3D view.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Piece weights are in the Puzzle tab.</strong> The W&minus; and W+ buttons are
              now in the Groups table, next to each piece.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The user guide has a new Piece Weights page and a Code Base section for developers.
            Many small bug fixes and improvements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/docs/piece-weights/">How piece weights work</a>
            ·
            <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/tag/v1.2">Release notes and downloads on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="post-v1-1">
          <p class="post-meta">
            <time datetime="2026-10-22">22 October 2026</time> · Release
          </p>
          <h3 id="post-v1-1">ShapeLogic v1.1: sliding puzzles with more than one layer</h3>
          <p>
            In sliding puzzles, pieces and trays can now have more than one layer. Before, a
            sliding shape was always flat, and a file with a deeper one was flattened when it
            opened. Now the layers are kept, and the <strong>Z</strong> size can be changed like
            on any other puzzle.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Pieces can move up and down.</strong> With Allow Vertical Moves on, a piece on
              a tray with more than one layer can be lifted out of a box, carried over the other
              pieces and put down somewhere else. The solver finds these moves, and the player
              shows them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Placing pieces by layer.</strong> Show a layer of the tray and click: the
              piece sits as low as it can while still fitting.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Flat puzzles are unchanged.</strong> Puzzles with one-layer trays are solved
              exactly as before, with the same answers.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The voxel editor&rsquo;s color area is also reworked, with clear Shape color and
            Restriction color labels, direct add, edit and remove buttons, and a built-in color
            picker. Spheres, rhombic and tetra-octa puzzles now default to Assemble Only, and the
            last shape in a puzzle can be deleted.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/docs/puzzle-types/sliding/#trays-with-more-than-one-layer">How multi-layer trays work</a>
            ·
            <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/tag/v1.1">Release notes and downloads on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="post-v1-0">
          <p class="post-meta">
            <time datetime="2026-10-19">19 October 2026</time> · Release
          </p>
          <h3 id="post-v1-0">ShapeLogic v1.0: the initial release</h3>
          <p>
            Today is the first public release of ShapeLogic, a designer and solver for interlocking,
            burr, sliding and stacking puzzles. It is a complete rewrite, with a new solver core, a
            new interface and a new documentation site.
          </p>
          <p>
            ShapeLogic is based on BurrTools, and its algorithms are ported from it. It reads and
            writes the same <code>.xmpuzzle</code> files. It is not meant to replace BurrTools.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>One interface everywhere.</strong> Built with web technologies and run in
              Electron, it behaves the same on macOS, Windows and Linux.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A fast solver.</strong> A C++23 engine runs as a separate process, so the
              interface stays responsive. Choose Complete (exhaustive search) or Fast (Andrew
              Crowell&rsquo;s take-apart).
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>New puzzle types.</strong> Sliding puzzles, stacking puzzles and Panex
              puzzles. Long Panex searches can be paused and resumed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Import and export.</strong> Import PuzzleSolver3D and Puzzlecad files, export
              to Puzzlecad, and export STL meshes for 3D printing. Cube-grid puzzles can be
              converted to rhombic and tetra-octa grids.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Well tested.</strong> Hundreds of unit and contract tests, end-to-end
              interface tests, and a harness that checks the solver&rsquo;s answers against the
              original on 180 puzzles.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Downloads are available for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel), Windows (64-bit) and Linux
            (x64 and ARM64). The macOS app is signed and notarised. The Windows installer is not
            code-signed yet, so SmartScreen may warn you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/shapelogicxyz/shapelogic/releases/tag/v1.0">Read the full release notes on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

    </main>

    <footer>
      <img src="/assets/puzzle_icon.jpg" alt="" width="40" height="40" />
      <p>
        ShapeLogic is free open-source software, built and maintained by Colin Deisenroth.<br />
        Contact: <a href="mailto:shapelogic.xyz@gmail.com">shapelogic.xyz@gmail.com</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
